ow to change the name of your file on github

To rename a file on GitHub, you can do it directly on the GitHub website or on your local machine using Git commands.

Method 1: On the GitHub Website (Fastest)
Navigate to the file you want to rename in your repository.

Click the Edit icon (pencil icon) in the top-right corner of the file preview.

In the filename field at the top, edit or replace the text to change the file name (you can also change the extension or path here).

Scroll down to the Commit changes... section.

Choose whether to commit directly to the current branch or create a new branch/pull request, then click Commit changes.

Method 2: On Your Local Machine (Using Git CLI)
If you have cloned the repository locally, use the git mv command so Git tracks the renaming history correctly:

Open your terminal or command line in the repository directory.

Run the git mv command specifying the old and new filenames:

Bash
git mv old_filename.py new_filename.py
Commit the change:

Bash
git commit -m "Rename old_filename.py to new_filename.py"
Push the update to GitHub:

Bash
git push origin main
(Replace main with your target branch name if different).
